{
  "id": "r18-filter-locus-replay",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Where could a repeat filter act?",
  "question": "Does the pinned seven-model source comparison distinguish filtering the ciphertext, keystream or plaintext from LP2 rune structure?",
  "reader_question": "Could a filter explain LP2’s scarce repeated runes?",
  "summary": "The exact seven-model, 200-replicate source run reproduces. Two output-stage rules remain hard to distinguish; several narrowly specified alternatives miss the manuscript measurements.",
  "finding": "The manuscript has 86 adjacent repeats among 12,955 pairs (0.664%). Simulated key skipping and random output rewriting give 0.633% and 0.668%. The source classifier has only 17.5% in-sample power to separate those two at its 5% false-positive threshold. A fixed +1 nudge matches repeats but produces a much larger modular-difference peak (16.5 versus 2.64 standardized units).",
  "limitations": "The source tunes suppression to the observed repeat rate and fits/evaluates its classifier on the same 200 simulations per model. This conditional comparison does not identify the manuscript encryptor or rule out other filters, keys, plaintexts or reset conventions.",
  "next_steps": "A mechanism claim needs an independently specified key or transition rule that predicts held-out runes or another unseen feature.",
  "outcome": "inconclusive",
